course: dictionary
lesson: 923
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the author means. The word bleep can be a noun (a short sound) or a verb (to make that sound). Look at the words around it to decide.
explain: The second sentence says, «"Bleeping" stands for any swear word, as if the word had been covered by a beep on TV.» The clue is TV and swear word, so bleeping here means hiding a rude word with a beep sound.
explain: Ask: which meaning fits this sentence? Then try it. If a machine gives a bleep, the word is the sound. If a doctor is bleeped, the word means called with a pager. The clues tell you which one to choose.
fact: A word with more than one meaning is called a word with several meanings.
fact: As a noun, «bleep» means a short, high electronic sound, like one from a machine or phone.
fact: As a verb, «bleep» means to make such a sound, for example a microwave bleeping when the food is ready.
fact: On TV or radio, a rude word can be bleeped out, which means covered with a bleep sound.
fact: In the UK, to bleep someone means to call them with a pager, such as a doctor.
exercise: «The microwave gave a loud bleep when the food was ready.» What does bleep mean here? | A kind of food | A doctor's phone call | A rude word | * A short, high electronic sound
exercise: On TV, the swear word was bleeped out. What does bleeped out mean? | Shouted very loudly | Written in big letters | Whispered quietly | * Covered with a beep sound
exercise: In the UK, the nurse bleeped the doctor to come to the ward. What does bleeped mean? | Painted the door | Took a photo of | * Called with a pager | Made a rude joke
test: «The alarm gave one bleep.» Is bleep a noun or a verb in this sentence? | * Noun | Adverb | Verb | Adjective
test: «Bleeping» stands for what, in the sentence about TV? | A type of robot | A happy song | A loud shout | * A swear word hidden by a beep
exam: «The lift kept bleeping, so Sam pressed the button again.» What does bleeping mean here? | Singing loudly | Stopping for ever | Calling someone on a pager | * Making short, high beeps
